<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">German Peasants' War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1524–1525</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The German Peasants' War was a widespread rising of peasants and townspeople in southern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in 1524–1525. Partly inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, the rebels' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelve-articles">Twelve Articles</a> demanded an end to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> and fair dues. Bands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-muntzer">Thomas Müntzer</a> preached, attacked castles and monasteries, but the princes' armies crushed them at Frankenhausen and elsewhere, killing perhaps 100,000 people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war.jpg" alt="German Peasants' War" width="320" height="328" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sansculotte at German Wikipedia Later versions were uploaded by TMA-1, Wombat at de.wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_bauernkrieg3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
